## Introduction
Cancer is one of the most significant public health problems worldwide and the second leading cause of death in the United States [1]. Nanotechnology has impacted cancer therapy over the past few decades by reducing the side effects such as neurotoxicity and normal tissue damage which are often observed in conventional cancer therapies such as chemotherapy and radiotherapy [2,3]. However, relatively few nanotherapeutics have been approved by the US Food and Drug Administration, and most of these nanomedicines do not significantly improve the efficacy of chemotherapeutic interventions [4]. Although tens of thousands of nanoparticles have been evaluated as potential cancer therapies, most of them are based on purely inorganic solids, such as quantum dots [5], $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [6], and Au nanoparticles [7], or purely organic materials, such as liposomes [8], dendrimers [9], and polymers [10]. The vast majority of these nanoparticles are designed to deliver a single chemotherapeutic agent, which would have to be combined with one or more other conventional chemotherapeutics to be effective against advanced tumors [11]. The relatively low drug loading efficiencies of many nanotherapeutics necessitate the use of a large amount of nanocarriers, which may introduce other side effects, such as infusion reactions, and compromise the pharmacokinetic and pharmacodyn amic properties of the delivered therapeutics [12]. There is thus a strong need to develop new nanoformulations that can efficiently combine multiple therapeutic modalities for effective cancer therapy.
Supramolecular metal-based nanoparticles (SMNPs) have recently been explored to overcome the aforementioned challenges [13]. Amorphous nanoscale coordination polymers (NCPs) and crystalline nanoscale metaleorganic frameworks (nMOFs) represent two most advanced SMNPs with at least three candidates in clinical trials (NCT03781362, NCT03953742, and NCT03444714). NCPs and nMOFs exploit the coordination polymerization between metals/metal clusters and organic/inorganic ligands to form the nanostructures [13,14]. Direct coordination polymerization of therapeutically relevant moieties significantly improves the loading efficiency to afford tunable and controllable nanotherapeutics [15,16]. More importantly, multiple organic and inorganic drugs of disparate physical and chemical properties can be combined in SMNPs by modifying organic ligands or coating with polymeric organic prodrugs to achieve synergistic effects. In this mini-review, we highlight recent advances in NCP- and nMOF-based supramolecular systems for cancer treatment.
## Loading therapeutic agents into SMNPs
Four different strategies have been developed to incorporate therapeutic agents into SMNPs [17]: (1) in the ligands; (2) on the metal clusters; (3) in the pores of porous nanostructures; (4) on the surfaces of the nanoparticles. A diverse range of therapeutic agents, including chemotherapeutic drugs [18,19], photosensitizers (PSs) [20], radioenhancers [21], peptides [22], and nucleic acids [23], have been incorporated into NCPs and nMOFs using these synthetic strategies. Recent efforts have been devoted to the modification of various therapeutics and their assembly into SMNPs to optimize physicochemical properties of these nanostructures and improve their anticancer efficacy. While this section discusses the loading of therapeutics into SMNPs in the ligands, on the metals, and in the pores, Section 3 examines the loading of therapeutic agents via surface modification of SMNPs.
## Ligands as therapeutics
Rieter et al [24] pioneered the rational design and biological application of using chemotherapeutics such as platinum-based drugs as the ligand in NCPs in the past decade. Liu et al. [19] prepared $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{IV})$ prodrugs of cisplatin and oxaliplatin with phosphonyl carbamate groups in the axial positions. The phosphate moiety allows coordination with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion to form an extended coordination polymer network which precipitates from the solution in the form of NCP particles. In vitro studies demonstrated the ability of NCPs to release parent platinum drugs upon internalization via an energy-dependent endocytosis and to induce effective DNA damage. In mouse xenograft models of nonsmallcell lung and pancreatic cancer, NCPs more effectively inhibited tumor growth than their parent Pt (II) drugs via systemic injection.
PSs have been incorporated into nMOFs as ligands for photodynamic therapy (PDT). In 2014, Lu et al [20] first reported a porphyrin-based nMOF as a highly effective PS for PDT. The same group has significantly advanced nMOF-based PSs to address a number of challenges in PDT in the past 5 years. For example, Luo et al. [25] recently reported that nMOF stabilized photochemically unstable bacteriochlorin PSs to realize type I and type II PDT. 5,10,15,20-tetra (p-benzoato) bacteriochlorin (TBB) is a labile PS with a short half-life of 1 min. With the geometrical constraint by the nMOF, the loaded TBB was well protected from photodegradation and $Z\mathrm{r-TBB}$ retained $64\%$ TBB in $30~\mathrm{min}$ (Figure $_{1\mathrm{a-c})}$ . The improved stability of $Z\mathrm{r-TBB}$ nMOF enhanced the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS) under the $740–\mathrm{nm}$ light irradiation, resulting in superb antitumor efficacy on 4T1 and MC38 tumor mouse models with cure rates of $40\%$ and $60\%$ , respectively.
## Metal clusters as therapeutics
High-Z metal and metal oxide nanoparticles have been explored as potential radiosensitizers [26e28]. Lin et al. demonstrated that high- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf Z}$ metal clusters could be incorporate into nMOFs for radiosensitization [21]. It was hypothesized that high-Z components could sensitize radiotherapy while the porous structures of nMOFs can facilitate the diffusion of ROS to lead to improved therapeutic effects [21,29]. Ni et al. [21] synthesized Hf-DBA $(\mathrm{DBA}=2{,}5\$ -di (p-benzoato)aniline) nMOFs by taking advantage of the electron-dense $\mathrm{Hf_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{14}$ secondary building units (SBUs) as X-ray absorbers to generate ROS and the open channels and small nMOF dimensions to facilitate ROS diffusion (Figure $\mathrm{1d\mathrm{-}f)}$ . These nMOFs showed much enhanced radiosensitization over $\mathrm{HfO}_{2}$ nanoparticles that have received approval from the European Medical Agency as a radiosensitizer for treating soft tissue sarcoma [30]. The nMOF-based radiotherapy works together with checkpoint blockade (anti-PD-L1 antibody) to successfully promote antitumor immune responses and regress both primary and distant tumors in several syngeneic mouse models.
## Therapeutics trapped in the pores
The porous structures of nMOFs have long been exploited for drug delivery [18,31e35]. Wang et al. [36] recently used MOF-199, a $\mathrm{Cu(II)}$ carboxylate-based metaleorganic framework (MOF), as an inert carrier to load PSs (Figure 1g). Intracellularly, $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ ions in the MOF effectively scavenge endogenous GSH and simultaneously decompose MOF-199 to allow the release of encapsulated PS for ROS generation. PSloaded MOF-199 showed highly effective in vitro cancer cell ablation and in vivo anticancer PDT with minimal normal cell phototoxicity.
## Surface modification of SMNPs
Liposomes and polymeric micelles have been shown to enhance drug circulation in the bloodstream [37]. Although SMNPs have shown effective cell uptake in vitro [38], their blood circulation property is not ideal [39]. Surface modification of SMNPs might endow long blood circulation by improving water dispersity, reducing plasma protein binding, and avoiding the mononuclear phagocytic system [40]. Attachment of affinity molecules on particle surfaces can further enhance tumor uptake via active targeting [17].
Surface modification can also slow down the degradation of SMNPs and prevent premature release of cargoes [41]. SMNPs have been coated with silica [24], lipid layers [42], nucleic acids [43], and others via electrostatic forceedriven self-assembly, hydrophobice hydrophobic interactions, or coordination with metals to improve their drug delivery efficiency.
## Lipid coating
The coating of SMNPs with lipids is motivated by the vast literature on liposomes which not only protect the delivered cargoes but also exhibit good pharmacokinetic properties [44]. Ploetz et al. [42] recently used lipidcoated MIL-(Fe) MOF nanoparticles to deliver iron ions to cells (Figure 2a). The lipids protected MIL-(Fe) from rapid degradation in the biological environments and afforded high cellular uptake of iron. Acidification in the tumor microenvironment led to intracellular degradation of MOF nanoparticles and release of iron. High concentration of iron ions in the cells activated caspases, induced the downregulation of gasdermin D by $70\%$ , and increased the release of $\mathrm{IL}{-}1\upbeta$ by 3 folds to cause pyroptosis, an inflammatory form of programmed cell death (Figure 2b and c).
## Cell membrane coating
Coating nanoparticles with cell membranes is an emerging approach to improve the in vivo performance of SMNPs [45]. In particular, nanoparticles coated with blood cell membranes were shown to effectively improve circulation after systemic administration [46]. This biomimetic strategy can endow the nanoparticles with the property of red blood cells, thereby reducing the elimination by the immune system [47]. For example, Zhang et al. [48] designed TPZ-GOx-ZIF$8@$ erythrocyte membrane for starvation-activated cancer therapy (Figure 2d).The nanosystem showed a ‘burst release’ behavior with $70\%$ drug release within $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $\mathrm{pH}5.0$ (Figure 2e). Only $10\%$ payload was released at $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ . By modulating the cancer cells into a highly hypoxic state with GOx and activating the hypoxiadependent prodrug tirapazamine, TPZ-GOx-ZIF$8@$ erythrocyte membrane inhibited tumor growth with a tumor growth inhibition (TGI) rate of $97.6\%$ (Figure 2f). Zhuang et al.[49] reported a platelet cell membraneecoated MOF delivery platform for the targeted delivery of siRNA in vivo. siRNA was introduced during MOF synthesis and loaded into the MOF core with high loading efficiency. The $\mathrm{pH}$ sensitivity of the MOF enabled endosomal disruption upon cellular uptake (Figure 3a). The cell membrane coating naturally promotes biointerfacing with disease substrates. Platelet cell membraneecoated MOF loaded with survivin siRNA enhanced the siRNA uptake by 7 folds over red blood cell membraneecoated MOF loaded with survivin siRNA 1 h after intravenous injection (Figure 3b). In SK-BR-3murine xenograft model, platelet cell membraneecoated MOF loaded with survivin siRNA showed significant antitumor efficacy and extended the median survival from 50 days to 92 days (Figure 3c).
## Drug loading via surface modification
The abnormal growth and metabolism of tumor cells lead to the development of unique physical and chemical microenvironments surrounding the tumor tissue, including acidic $\mathrm{pH}$ , hypoxia, high levels of glutathione (GSH) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and overexpression of certain proteins [50]. Although these microenvironmental changes favor tumor proliferation, invasion, adhesion, and drug resistance [51], they can also provide endogenous stimuli to design tumor-specific nanomedicines. Surface modification of SMNPs have allowed the introduction of stimuli-responsive groups such as imidazolyl $\left(\mathrm{pH}\right)$ [52] and disulfide bonds (GSH) [53] to facilitate conformational changes and drug releases in tumor microenvironments [54].
Covalently attached drugs can be selectively released at low pH via hydrolysis of acid-labile bonds between the drug and the nanoparticle. The pH-triggered bond cleavage can be finely controlled with properly selected linkers. Several pH-sensitive linkers, such as ether [55], hydrazine [56], and Schiff base [57], have been used for covalent drug loading and triggered release. Zhang et al.
[57] designed a series of multifunctional MOF nanoparticles (MCH NPs) based on MIL-100 $\mathrm{(Fe)}$ for pHresponsive drug delivery. These particles contained pHsensitive polydopamine-modified hyaluronic acid as the shell and MIL-100 encapsulating curcumin as the core (Figure 3b). The MCH NPs showed high drug loading $(94.3\%)$ and pH-responsive drug release; $62\%$ drug was released in $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $\mathrm{pH}=5.6$ . In vivo studies with HeLa tumor-bearing mice showed that the MCH NPs achieved tumor ablation from the local hyperthermia $(\sim55^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ in $10~\mathrm{\min}$ under $808–\mathrm{nm}$ laser (1.0 $\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , $10~\mathrm{\min}$ irradiation).
## Targeted delivery to specific organelles in cancer cells
Surface coating with specific targeting molecules can be exploited to enhance drug delivery efficiency and reduce systemic toxicity [58,59]. Haddad et al. [58] modified the surface of $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -NMOF, UiO-66, with triphenylphosphonium (TPP) to afford a mitochondria targeting nMOF. The lipophilic cations of TPP facilitated the accumulation of nMOFs on the mitochondria, resulting in the intramitocontrial release of the chemotherapy dichloroacetate preloaded into nMOFs to induce apoptosis. Several nMOFs with intrinsic mitochondria targeting ability without relying on TPP have been developed and shown to significantly enhance antitumor efficacy in a number of mouse tumor models [60,61].
## SMNPs with multiple synergistic functional modalities
Combining different therapeutic modalities in one SMNP can have synergistic effects to improve efficacy. Three different approaches have been developed to combine different therapeutic modalities in SMNPs:
(1) codelivering multiple chemotherapeutic agents for synergistic efficacy; (2) incorporating therapeutic metals and ligands within one nanostructure; (3) codelivering different therapeutic modalities, including chemotherapeutics, PSs, radioenhancers, and biologics, for synergistic effects.
## Codelivery of chemotherapeutic agents
Combination chemotherapies are used as first- and second-line therapies for many cancers [62]. For example, the FOLFININOX regimen consisting of folinic acid, fluorouracil, irinotecan, and oxaliplatin is the preferred first-line treatment for patients with metastatic pancreatic cancer and good performance status [63]. Nanoparticles loaded with multiple chemotherapeutic agents have shown improved efficacy with reduced side effects [2]. In 2017, a liposomal formulation encapsulating both cytarabine and daunorubicin (Vyxeos $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ ) was approved by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for the treatment of acute myeloid leukemia [64]. With chemical tunability, SMNPs have been designed to codeliver multiple chemotherapeutic agents via surface modifications. For example, Duan et al. [53] developed coreeshell OxPt/DHA NCPs carrying oxaliplatin in the core and dihydroartemisinin (DHA) in the shell and showed that $\mathrm{{OxPt/DHA}}$ synergistically induced immunogenetic cell death and enhanced antitumor efficacy in subcutaneous mouse tumor models (Figure 4a and b). The released DHA induced ROS generation in tumor cells, causing exposure of calreticulin and secretion of damageassociated molecular patterns (DAMPs) to enhance antigen presentation cell recruitment (Figure 4c). $\mathrm{OxPt}/\$ DHA in combination with anti-PD-L1 eradicated CT26 colon tumors and achieved long-term antitumoral immunity for more than 3 months.
## Metal clusters and ligands for synergistic effects
Metal clusters and ligands can be incorporated into nMOFs in periodic arrays to achieve synergistic effects. Lu et al. [29] first incorporated high-Z metal clusters and porphyrin PSs into nMOFs to develope a new X-rayactivated therapeutic modality termed radiotherapye radiodynamic therapy (Figure 4d). 5,15-di (p-benzoato)- porphyrin-Hf and 5,10,15,20-tetra (p-benzoato)- porphyrin-Hf were designed and evaluated for their X-ray radiosensitization abilities. The high-Z Hf-oxo clusters in nMOFs show strong X-ray attenuation to sensitize RT via enhanced production of OH while some of the absorbed energy can be transferred to porphyrins to enable RDT by sensitizing the generation of ${}^{1}\mathrm{\dot{O}}_{2}$ . An indoleamine 2,3- dioxygenase inhibitor was further loaded into the pores and channels of 5,15-di(p-benzoato)-porphyrin-Hf to combine radiotherapyeradiodynamic therapy with cancer immunotherapy (Figure 4e and f).
Lan et al. [65] designed a titanium nMOF, Tie 5,10,15,20-tetra(p-benzoato)porphyrin (TBP), based on
TBP bridging ligands. Upon light irradiation, singlet oxygen was produced from photoexcited TBP. In the meantime, the excitation energy was transferred to Tioxo SBUs, partially reducing $\mathrm{Ti(IV)}$ to Ti(III) without changing the coordination environment and the nanostructures. The Ti(III) centers propagated the generation of superoxide $(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-})$ ), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and $\bullet\mathrm{OH}$ for type I PDT. In vivo efficacy study on a hypoxic CT26 tumor mouse model showed that TieTBP-mediated PDT inhibited tumor growth with TGI of $98.4\%$ and a cure rate of $60\%$ .
## Codelivery of multiple therapeutic modalities
He et al. [66] developed a series of NCPs by incorporating cisplatin and gemcitabine as coordination ligands with zinc ions within the core while noncovalent attachment of siRNAs targeting survivin, Bcl-2, and ERCC-1 to the shell using DSPE-siRNA conjugates. The siRNAs were conjugated with DSPE by disulfide bonds and could be released from NCPs in phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) supplemented with $10\ \mathrm{mM}$ GSH (to mimic the intracellular environment). The anticancer efficacy of NCP/siRNAs was demonstrated in vivo against SKOV-3 subcutaneous xenografts; NCP/siRNAs completely eradicated the tumors, and the mice remained tumor free for at least 156 days.
Hu et al. [67] used NCPs to codeliver nitric oxide (NO) and iron ions to induce chemodynamic therapy. The nanoparticles were constructed by coordination between a GSH-sensitive NO donor, 1,5-bis [(L-proline-1-yl) diazen-1-ium-1,2-diol- $O^{2}$ -yl]-2,4-dinitrobenzene and iron ions (Figure 5a). High concentrations of GSH in tumor cells triggered NO release from the NCP (Figure 5b). In the meantime, $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ions underwent Fenton reaction to generate OH in the tumor environment with high $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration. Furthermore, $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ induced the generation of $\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ by the HabereWeiss reaction, which quickly reacted with NO to afford peroxynitrite anion for enhanced cytotoxicity. In vivo study showed that this NO chemodynamic therapy effect retarded the growth of Heps xenograft tumors in Institute of Cancer Research (ICR) mouse models with $\sim60\%$ TGI (Figure 5c).
He et al. [68] designed a nanostructure for triggered release of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as and chemoradiotherapy (Figure 5d) by in situ growing gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) on the surface of MOF to afford MOF-Au nanocomplex. AuNPs acted as radioenhancers for radiotherapy and formed an artificial enzyme with MOF to convert $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figure 5e). MOF-Au was further loaded with doxorubicin (Dox) to realize chemoradiotherapy. The enhanced anticancer efficacy was observed in vitro and in vivo with negligible systemic toxicity (Figure 5f).
## Conclusions
SMNPs have witnessed significant progress in the past few years and, in particular, have evolved from delivering single therapeutic agents to codelivering multiple therapeutic agents and therapeutic modalities by leveraging their unique chemical structures. Therapeutic agents can be incorporated into SMNPs as ligands, on metal clusters, in pores and channels, and on surfaces. SMNPs exhibit efficient cellular uptake such as inorganic nanomaterials but also possess synthetic tunability of organic nanomaterials, leading to excellent anticancer efficacy in vitro and in vivo. By combining a broad range of therapeutic strategies, including chemotherapy, radiotherapy, phototherapy, and immunotherapy, in a single SMNP, researchers have achieved synergistic therapeutic effects to overcome drug resistance and to reduce general toxicity. We believe advanced SMNPs will be developed in coming years to address unmet needs in cancer therapy.
